1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,347
I built a thousand systems with no edge at all. Just like flipping a coin.

2
00:00:11,798 --> 00:00:18,146
Then I backtested them all. The best one made +32R in a hundred trades.

3
00:00:18,596 --> 00:00:23,585
Live, it will earn nothing. This trap is called overfitting.

4
00:00:24,585 --> 00:00:29,496
Hello, this is episode nine of the SPIDER Quant Trading series.

5
00:00:29,945 --> 00:00:35,275
Last time: the system report card. Profit factor and Sharpe.

6
00:00:35,725 --> 00:00:40,871
Today: overfitting. When a backtest looks better than the truth.

7
00:00:41,871 --> 00:00:48,950
Market data holds two things: a real pattern, and noise, the random wiggles.

8
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:54,651
A real pattern can show up again. Noise never repeats the same way.

9
00:00:55,101 --> 00:01:01,292
Overfitting means the system memorised the noise and mistook it for a pattern.

10
00:01:02,292 --> 00:01:07,621
A student memorised last year's exam paper. A hundred out of a hundred on that paper.

11
00:01:08,071 --> 00:01:14,758
A new paper came, and he failed. He learnt answers, not the subject.

12
00:01:15,208 --> 00:01:21,006
An overfit system does the same. Top of the class on old data, fails on new data.

13
00:01:22,006 --> 00:01:24,933
It usually happens by tweaking settings again and again.

14
00:01:25,383 --> 00:01:33,350
20 moving average lengths, 10 stop sizes, 5 sessions. A thousand combinations in total.

15
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:42,550
Run them all, pick the best. The more combinations, the more likely the best one is pure luck.

16
00:01:43,550 --> 00:01:54,000
Look at the hook experiment. Each system: 100 trades, half wins, half losses, win 1R, loss 1R. Zero edge.

17
00:01:54,449 --> 00:02:00,720
By luck some end up, some end down. Most sit near zero.

18
00:02:01,169 --> 00:02:13,682
But the best of a thousand: about +32R. 66 wins, 34 losses, a profit factor near 1.9. And that number means nothing.

19
00:02:14,682 --> 00:02:18,653
Sign one: many rules, few trades.

20
00:02:19,102 --> 00:02:26,992
Every rule or setting is a dial you can turn. More dials make it easier to fit old data.

21
00:02:27,442 --> 00:02:33,162
Ten dials and only forty trades? That system is not learning, it is memorising.

22
00:02:34,162 --> 00:02:39,309
Sign two: brilliant on one setting, bad on its neighbours.

23
00:02:39,759 --> 00:02:52,585
System A: +42R at moving average 50. But −6R at 45 and −4R at 55. A sharp spike, just luck.

24
00:02:53,035 --> 00:03:03,222
System B: +12 to +18R on every setting from 30 to 70. A plateau. Trust that kind more.

25
00:03:04,222 --> 00:03:07,697
Sign three: a result that looks too good.

26
00:03:08,147 --> 00:03:18,100
An almost straight equity line, profit factor above 3, no bad month. As we said last episode: get suspicious first.

27
00:03:18,549 --> 00:03:23,226
A real edge is usually small, and the path is bumpy.

28
00:03:24,226 --> 00:03:30,260
Fix one: keep part of the data hidden. Remember episode five?

29
00:03:30,710 --> 00:03:36,666
Build the system on 70% of the data. Test on the other 30% only once.

30
00:03:37,116 --> 00:03:52,189
System A: profit factor 2.4 inside, 0.95 outside. Overfit. System B: 1.5 inside, 1.4 outside. That one holds.

31
00:03:53,189 --> 00:03:56,192
Fix two: the walk-forward test.

32
00:03:56,643 --> 00:04:05,733
Pick settings on 12 months. Test on the next 3. Then slide the whole window 3 months forward, and repeat.

33
00:04:06,183 --> 00:04:14,307
Join all the test pieces. That is the honest result, because every piece was new to the system.

34
00:04:15,307 --> 00:04:20,793
Fix three: keep the system simple. Few rules, few settings.

35
00:04:21,243 --> 00:04:31,274
And run it on different markets and periods. Gold, currencies, indices. If it works a little everywhere, the edge is more likely real.

36
00:04:31,724 --> 00:04:36,635
And remember: the more combinations you tried, the more suspicious you should be.

37
00:04:37,635 --> 00:04:39,202
So, today's summary.

38
00:04:39,652 --> 00:04:46,340
Overfitting: the system memorised noise. Top on old data, fails on new.

39
00:04:46,790 --> 00:04:53,947
Signs: few trades, many dials, a sharp spike, and a result that looks too good.

40
00:04:54,397 --> 00:05:00,353
Fixes: hidden data, walk-forward, a simple system, and many markets.

41
00:05:01,353 --> 00:05:07,962
Next episode: your first system. Writing the rules, and a backtest in Excel.

42
